// Evergrove's match-three levels. SOE's level tables never shipped with the client, so every number here is ours. The
// fields are explained in src/Sanctuary.Game/Minigames/MatchThree/MatchThreeDefinition.cs and docs/research/mining.md.
//
// "Name" is the client's own name for the game (the Flash's GameData.sGAME), which picks the pictures; "GameNumber" is
// SOE's number for it (the Flash's ConfigServer.GAME_ID), which picked its rows in SOE's lost level tables.
//
// What each game's Flash can draw (a type it can't draw shows a blank or a wrong picture):
// - mining: rocks 1 to 5; ore 0-2 iron, 3-5 tin, 6-8 silver, 9-11 gold, 12-14 copper (low, medium, high quality);
//   gems 0 tourmaline, 1 topaz, 2 onyx, 3 moonstone, 4 turquoise, 5 amethyst, 6 garnet, 7 ruby, 8 fire agate,
//   9 malachite. A bin's label is the client's string "miningValue<value>", and SOE's are one off from the pictures:
//   601-603 say Iron, 604-606 Tin, 607-609 Silver, 610-612 Gold, 613-614 Copper. So copper ore is 13 or 14.
// - farming: crops 1 to 16 (1 Gummyfruit, 2 Snowberry, 3 Spiralmint, 4 Moonbean, 5 Choychoy, 6 Sunleaf, 7 Honeytwig,
//   8 Punkin, 9 Briarmelon, 10 Rainleaf, 11 Stickfruit, 12 Starnut, 13 Stainberry, 14 Chugaroot, 15 Bumbleberry,
//   16 Tumtumnip). No ore or gems: the bins count the crops the player chains.
// - mail: letters 1 to 8 (1 Sealed Envelope, 2 Blue Envelope, 3 Box with Twine, 4 Gift Box, 5 Stamped Envelope,
//   6 Opened Envelope, 7 Scroll, 8 Box). No ore or gems: the bins count the letters the player chains.
// - forging: metals 1 to 5; weapon pieces 0 to 26 (0 Sword, 1 Armor, 2 Chest, 3 Halberd, 4 Katana, 5 Hammer, 6 Pickaxe,
//   7 Pan, 8 Wand, 9 Reflex, 10 Hockey, 11 Baseball, 12 Crowbar, 13 Bow, 14 Bonesaw, 15 Scalpel, 16 Scythe,
//   17 Chest2, 18 Potions, 19 Feet, 20 Rune, 21 Housing, 22 Hands, 23 Head, 24 Legs, 25 Shoulders, 26 Pets); no gems.
//   Two bins only, and a mold meter instead of bin labels: MoldId N is the mold of weapon piece N - 1 (14 is the bow),
//   and it fills by 100 / (the level's total quota) per piece, so keep that total a divisor of 100.
//
// Where the board appears: the Flash draws cell (x, y) at (40x, 40y) in its game clip and never centres the board, so
// the cells a grid uses decide where the board sits in its frame. SOE's boards covered columns 1 to 12, and the frame,
// bins and meters are drawn around them, so a board must be centred on column 6.5 (columns 3 to 10 for 8 wide; the
// server refuses a level that isn't). Mining, Farming and Forging use rows 2 to 10 (drop zone in row 10), inside the
// rows SOE's boards covered (1 to 10 in Mining, 2 to 11 in the others); Mail Sorting uses rows 1 to 9, because its
// bins start 427 pixels down its game clip, which row 10 would overlap.
//
// Power-ups: 0 more time, 1 double score, 2 five times score, 50 dynamite, 51 wild tiles, 52 destruct, 53 shuffle. None
// of the four games has a picture for 1 or 2 (they show the first power-up picture instead), so the levels leave them
// out. More time falls out through the drop zone like ore, which is why every board keeps a drop-zone row.
